<article class="env-lemma" id="052V">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/052V">Lemma <span data-tag="052V">10.124.2</span>.</a> Let $(R, \mathfrak m_ R) \to (S, \mathfrak m_ S)$ be a local homomorphism of local rings. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$R \to S$ is essentially of finite type, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\kappa (\mathfrak m_ R) \subset \kappa (\mathfrak m_ S)$ is finite, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\dim (S/\mathfrak m_ RS) = 0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $S$ is the localization of a finite $R$-algebra. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $S'$ be a finite type $R$-algebra such that $S = S'_{\mathfrak q'}$ for some prime $\mathfrak q'$ of $S'$. By Definition <a href="/tag/00PL" data-tag="00PL">10.122.3</a> we see that $R \to S'$ is quasi-finite at $\mathfrak q'$. After replacing $S'$ by $S'_{g'}$ for some $g' \in S'$, $g' \not\in \mathfrak q'$ we may assume that $R \to S'$ is quasi-finite, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00QA" data-tag="00QA">10.123.13</a>. Then by Lemma <a href="/tag/00QB" data-tag="00QB">10.123.14</a> there exists a finite $R$-algebra $S''$ and elements $g' \in S'$, $g' \not\in \mathfrak q'$ and $g'' \in S''$ such that $S'_{g'} \cong S''_{g''}$ as $R$-algebras. This proves the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>